# Latu quick start

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:latu, "~> 0.7"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
])
```

## A server to talk to

Latu builds a query plan in this notebook and Spark runs it. There is no JVM in the runtime
and no cluster on this machine. You need a Spark Connect server, and nothing else.

Point at a cluster's `sc://` URL, or start one here:

```bash
docker run -p 15002:15002 apache/spark:4.2.0 /opt/spark/sbin/start-connect-server.sh --wait
```

```elixir
url = Kino.Input.text("Spark Connect URL", default: "sc://localhost:15002")
```

A session is a plain struct wrapping a gRPC channel. Nothing joins the supervision tree.

```elixir
session = Latu.connect!(Kino.Input.read(url))

Latu.spark_version!(session)
```

## Some data

Verbs are called qualified, the way `Enum` is. Expressions come from `Latu.Column`, which is
small enough to import, and Spark's function library is aliased as `F`.

`Latu.create_dataframe!/2` ships local rows to the cluster as Arrow. A frame renders as the
table Spark draws for it, so the last line of a cell is enough to look at one.

```elixir
import Latu.Column
alias Latu.Functions, as: F

df =
  Latu.create_dataframe!(session, [
    %{id: 1, line: "Latu builds a plan"},
    %{id: 2, line: "Spark runs the plan on a cluster"},
    %{id: 3, line: "the plan is just data"}
  ])
```

## Filter, and look before you run

A verb is a pure function from one plan to the next, so nothing has run yet. Inspecting a frame
shows the plan's spine and costs no round trip; rendering it runs the query.

```elixir
about_spark = Latu.filter(df, contains(:line, "Spark"))

IO.inspect(about_spark, label: "plan")

about_spark
```

## Word count

Spark's own quick start ends with MapReduce, and it is the same three verbs here.
`Latu.count/1` on a grouped frame is lazy, a transformation that adds a `count` column.

```elixir
counts =
  df
  |> Latu.select(word: F.explode(F.split(:line, "\\s+")))
  |> Latu.group_by(:word)
  |> Latu.count()
  |> Latu.sort([desc(:count)])
```

## Bring it back

An action returns `{:ok, value}` or `{:error, %Latu.Error{}}`, and every action has a `!` twin.
Rows come back as maps with atom keys, or as an Explorer frame, which Livebook renders as a
table of its own.

```elixir
Latu.collect!(counts)
```

```elixir
Latu.to_explorer!(counts)
```

## Disconnect

Closing the channel leaves the server's own session to time out. `Latu.release_session/2` ends
it now.

```elixir
Latu.disconnect!(session)
```

